===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|scon|scōn|lang=gmh}}, {{term|scone|scōne|beautifully, appropriately, completely|lang=gmh}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|scono|scōno|beautifully|lang=goh}}; cognate with {{term|schön||beautiful|lang=de}}.<ref>[[w:Friedrich Kluge|Kluge, Friedrich]] (1975). ''Etymologisches Wörterbuch der deutschen Sprache''. 21st edition. Berlin: Walter de Gruyter. p.&nbsp;675.</ref>

====Adverb====
{{de-adv}}

# [[already]]; [[yet]]
# [[surely]]; [[indeed]]
#: {{usex|Ja, '''schon'''|t=Yes, indeed.|lang=de|inline=1}}

=====Usage notes=====
''Schon'' is used more frequently than its English equivalents.  It is often used to emphasize agreement or the earliness of an action. With reference to the passage of time, the opposite of ''schon'' is {{term|erst|lang=de}}. While ''schon'' emphasizes how soon something will happen, ''erst'' emphasizes how long it is until something happens. Thus ''schon in drei Wochen'' means "in only three weeks [and I'm glad I don't have to wait longer!]", while ''erst in drei Wochen'' means "not for three weeks [and that seems so far away]".

